PUBLIC RESPONSE: The correct choice is B. In the well-known chemotopic mapping of the rodent olfactory bulb (e.g., from 2-DG and optical-imaging studies by Johnson, Leon, and colleagues), aliphatic odorants that share a functional group are represented along a roughly anteroposterior axis in which preferred carbon-chain length systematically increases from rostral to caudal. Consequently, molecules with longer chains activate glomeruli situated more posteriorly, while shorter-chain homologs activate more anterior sites. Options A, D, and E reverse or misplace this axis; option C states an equivalent fact but is phrased for short-chain molecules and is therefore not the conventional formulation of the finding.

(The question stem appears truncated, but the standard empirical generalization matches B exactly.)

}

